--TEST--
Core\Storage reaches only a disk an operator configured, so a name no `[storage.<name>]` block carries is refused with nothing to fall back to — ADR 0082 § 2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The whole of ADR 0082 § 2's storage rule, from the outside: `$disk` selects a
// block in root-owned `nvs.toml`, and there is no root, path or directory
// parameter anywhere on the three rows. So a program running under a
// deployment that named no such disk cannot store at all — there is nothing to
// fall back to, and no argument through which it could supply one.
//
// The deployment below configures `uploads` and not `archive`, which is what
// makes this a refusal about the *name* rather than about a tree with no
// storage configured at all.
try {
    Core\Storage::put("archive", "note.txt", "x" as bytes);
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Core\Storage::get("archive", "note.txt");
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Core\Storage::delete("archive", "note.txt");
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}

// The disk is answered before the key, so a program that named neither
// correctly learns about the disk first: there is no key that could be right
// on a disk that does not exist.
try {
    Core\Storage::get("archive", "../escape");
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[storage.uploads]
root = "./objects"

[capabilities.fs]
read = ["./objects"]
write = ["./objects"]
--EXPECT--
Core\Storage::put: no `[storage.archive]` block sets `root`, so there is no disk of that name to store on
Core\Storage::get: no `[storage.archive]` block sets `root`, so there is no disk of that name to store on
Core\Storage::delete: no `[storage.archive]` block sets `root`, so there is no disk of that name to store on
Core\Storage::get: no `[storage.archive]` block sets `root`, so there is no disk of that name to store on
